  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes nitrogen cycling driven by different microbial groups across several marine and plant-associated environments. Across all panels, arrows connect named bacteria and archaea to key nitrogen compounds such as ammonium, nitrite, nitrate, organic nitrogen, nitrous oxide, and dinitrogen, showing how microbial communities transform nitrogen through fixation, nitrification, denitrification, anammox, and recycling.\n\nThe upper marine panels emphasize planktonic systems. Cyanobacteria and associated microbes appear to supply fixed nitrogen, while abundant ocean taxa such as Pelagibacter, Prochlorococcus, and nitrifiers like Nitrosopumilus and Nitrospina exchange ammonium, nitrite, and nitrate. Organic matter release, grazing, and viral lysis feed organic nitrogen back into the system. A second marine scheme highlights low-oxygen conditions, where pathways leading to N2 and N2O become more prominent and involve groups such as Scalindua, Arcobacter, and other proteobacteria.\n\nThe lower panels shift to soils or rhizosphere-like settings. Around roots, organic carbon inputs stimulate complex coupling between aerobic and anaerobic processes. Nitrifiers convert ammonium to nitrite and nitrate in oxic zones, while denitrifiers, anammox bacteria, and methane-linked microbes in anoxic zones return nitrogen to N2 or N2O. Overall, the image conveys that nitrogen transformations depend strongly on habitat, oxygen availability, and interactions among diverse microbial taxa.",
